The Model Context Protocol just shipped its biggest revision since it
launched, and it's not an incremental one. The 2026-07-28 spec drops
protocol-level sessions entirely, and if you're running MCP servers in
production the way we are for our agent framework, this is a genuine
migration, not a changelog to skim.

## Sessions are gone, and that's the whole point

Every MCP interaction used to start with a handshake: negotiate capabilities,
get a session ID back, and keep that session pinned to one server instance
for the life of the connection. That pinning is exactly what made MCP servers
annoying to run at any scale — you needed sticky sessions, shared session
storage, or both.

The new spec kills the handshake. Every request now carries its own protocol
version, client identity, and capabilities inline. A single tool call is
fully self-contained — no server-side session to look up, no particular
instance it has to land on.

## What that actually buys you

- **Round-robin load balancing works again.** No sticky sessions, no shared
  session store. A plain load balancer in front of a Kubernetes deployment is
  enough.
- **Servers can ask mid-operation without holding a stream open.** Multi
  round-trip requests replace the old long-lived bidirectional connection for
  things like missing-parameter prompts or confirmations.
- **Gateways can route without parsing bodies.** Method and tool names now
  travel in headers, so a gateway can authorize and route a request without
  touching the JSON payload.
- **Tool and resource listings are cacheable.** They now carry TTL and
  cache-scope hints instead of forcing a fresh call every time.

## The part that'll actually cost you time

Tasks — the mechanism for long-running operations — got pulled out of core
and into a formal extension, alongside MCP Apps and Enterprise Managed
Authorization. If anything in your stack depended on Tasks being part of the
base protocol, that's a real code change, not a version bump.

There's a twelve-month deprecation window for what's removed, and old and new
aren't guaranteed to talk to each other. All four Tier-1 SDKs — TypeScript,
Python, Go, C# — already support it, with Rust in beta.

## Where we're landing

Our agent framework runs its own MCP servers behind Kubernetes, and the
session-affinity dance was never fun to operate. Losing it is a
straightforward win. The Tasks extraction is the part we're actually
budgeting time for — anything built assuming Tasks was baked into core needs
a real look before we touch the new spec in production.
